Ray Dillinger <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
>
> Yeah.  Identical ascension kits bother me too, although it's only 
> one symptom of the problem that I focus on.  That problem I
> call "thousanduplet syndrome" and it consists of winning characters 
> all looking too much alike, as though some poor woman somewhere had 
> given birth to identical thousanduplets  (and a complicated and 
> painful labor it must have been, since all the kids are armored and 
> have large pointy things with them). By endgame, virtually all
> characters have the same set of capabilities, or very near to it.  

If you allow permanent resistances almost all players want to and will
get them. There are just too many dangerous monsters in NetHacks
endgame if you miss some vital resistances.

I think endgame characters also all look the same because you can
bring down your AC to almost ludicrous levels.

Maybe the enchantment should not be limited at 5/7 but at 4/6 or maybe
even 3/5.

This would also lead to a more differentiated endgame. You would have
to rely more on your abilities (jump, speed, charm monsters, ...) than
on your AC.

> Crawl IMO does a much better job of avoiding thousanduplet syndrome, 
> largely because of the design of its skill set. 

Could give a short description of Crawls skill set? I don't know a lot
about Crawl and I'm probably not the only one.
  
